@startuml 03_why_atomics_exist
title 3. Why atomic operations were invented
skinparam backgroundColor #FFFFFF
skinparam roundcorner 12
left to right direction

rectangle "1960s\nPrograms start sharing memory.\nDijkstra's semaphores: mutual exclusion\nas a software concept" as A1 #E8EEF7
rectangle "1970\nIBM System/370 adds a compare-and-swap\ninstruction: the hardware makes one\nread-modify-write indivisible" as A2 #E8EEF7
rectangle "Late 1970s onward\nIntel x86 adds the LOCK prefix\n(lock add, lock cmpxchg)\nthe same idea in every PC today" as A3 #E8EEF7
rectangle "1991\nHerlihy shows CAS can build\nany lock-free data structure\n(wait-free synchronization)" as A4 #E8EEF7
rectangle "2011\nC++11 standardizes std::atomic\nand a memory model\n(what this project uses)" as A5 #E8EEF7

A1 -right-> A2
A2 -right-> A3
A3 -right-> A4
A4 -right-> A5

footer Why: software-only locks need ordering guarantees that modern CPUs do not give, so the hardware had to provide indivisible instructions.
@enduml
